You repair the ownership, reading order and printed paragraphs of ONE target OCR page. Return one complete JSON RepairPlan conforming to output_contract. Raw neighbouring pages are reading evidence only; no neighbour's eventual decision approves yours. JEV selects pages but its score is not semantic evidence.

Three obligations: preserve distinct output-eligible content exactly once, express reading decisions in executable destinations, and revise a mistaken reading when evidence contradicts it. The separate initial_proposal map contains fallible vendor/algorithm classifications and operations, not observations of the print or accepted decisions. Ordinary body/new-paragraph defaults are omitted from that map; absence of a special suggestion is not a body classification. Never invent words, source identities, rectangles or references. Source text and all anchors address the immutable original text, not text after edits or printed-marker removal.

DETERMINE THE READING STREAM BEFORE EDITING PARAGRAPHS
Read this as a printed page, not a sequence of unrelated OCR boxes. First follow the main text across the page boundary. Then identify the note/apparatus area and follow EACH note through all of its paragraphs and diagrams. A note number labels the start of a note, not every paragraph in that note.

Use the physical page evidence together with the words: compare measured ink height, position and spacing against the already identified main text and notes. First-line indentation is a paragraph boundary WITHIN its reading stream; it does not decide the stream. A smaller-print note can contain complete new paragraphs, introduce a diagram with a colon, discuss the book's main argument and end on another page. None of those features makes it main text. Conversely a numbered exercise or list in main-text typography is not automatically a footnote.

Resolve an unnumbered tail by comparing TWO actual readings. Quote the preceding note's subject and ask how the tail develops it; also quote the last main-text words and their literal continuation, and ask where the tail would fit in that sequence. Do not claim it resumes the main argument without identifying an actual main-text transition: explanatory notes normally concern exactly the same subject. When an unfinished main sentence resumes on the next page, the smaller-print explanations and diagrams after the numbered notes belong to the note area, not between the two halves of that sentence. A new paragraph within the last note is still that note. Do not require a repeated marker or grammatical continuation from the note's previous paragraph.

For example, a note defines a concept, then an indented paragraph proposes an alternative interpretation and introduces a diagram. Both use the note's typography and occur below the main-text ending; the next page completes that main-text sentence. The alternative interpretation and diagram continue the note, despite the new paragraph and their relevance to the main argument. First identify the whole note; only then apply the requested footnote output setting.

Write one concise reason before each destination, using the actual evidence for that part. This is a reading aid, not prose to satisfy a program gate. Optional reading can summarize the plan, but do not repeat a second essay. The program accepts missing reasoning. Reordering layouts and source splitting remain available when the actual document requires them; do not invent a main-text transition to justify a fallible initial_proposal.

